{"authored" [{"question" "q-280670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the volume $V$ of the parallelepiped spanned by $x=⟨1,2,3⟩$, $y=⟨0,1,-1⟩$, and $z=⟨2,0,1⟩.$\n\n$V=${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd388d00a70ed291b41e763546f24dc223c781c7cc6900097248a4b716cf78ea" "basis" 1398 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-280670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The volume of the parallelepiped spanned by three vectors is given by the absolute value of their triple product.\n\nFirst, we compute the triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (y\\times z) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|+3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 0)-2(0\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot 2)+3(0\\cdot 0-1\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =1-4-6 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nFinally, we take the absolute value. So, the volume of the corresponding parallelepiped is \n\n$$\nV=|-9|=9.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd388d00a70ed291b41e763546f24dc223c781c7cc6900097248a4b716cf78ea" "basis" 1398 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-280670" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1eb67db518936ec2db7e8202c1597822f37f8d6e6f051fdbf9470bb1bc3f378" "basis" 1398 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-280670" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd388d00a70ed291b41e763546f24dc223c781c7cc6900097248a4b716cf78ea" "basis" 1398 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find $k$ given that the vectors $a=⟨k,1,4⟩,$ $b=⟨8,0,3⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,1,1⟩$ are coplanar." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd388d00a70ed291b41e763546f24dc223c781c7cc6900097248a4b716cf78ea" "basis" 1398 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Computing the triple product, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =|\\begin{aligned}k & 1 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =k\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+4\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =k(0\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3)-(8\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 3)+4(8\\cdot 1-3\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =-3k+1+32 \\\\  & =33-3k \\\\  & =3(11-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nThree vectors are coplanar if and only if their triple product is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =0 \\\\ 3(11-k) & =0 \\\\ 11-k & =0 \\\\ k & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the three vectors are coplanar if and only if $k=11.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd388d00a70ed291b41e763546f24dc223c781c7cc6900097248a4b716cf78ea" "basis" 1398 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128436" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f1eb67db518936ec2db7e8202c1597822f37f8d6e6f051fdbf9470bb1bc3f378" "basis" 1398 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd388d00a70ed291b41e763546f24dc223c781c7cc6900097248a4b716cf78ea" "basis" 1398 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "11"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd388d00a70ed291b41e763546f24dc223c781c7cc6900097248a4b716cf78ea" "basis" 1398 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128436" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "14"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14040011/state.json" "file_sha256" "bd388d00a70ed291b41e763546f24d